2026-08-24 — THE HEIGHT WAS A MEASUREMENT OF 2010.

`hgt_mediancm` is the median cell of (first return minus bare earth) over each
footprint. The dataset's own methodology document, SF_BldgFoot_2017-05, says
both grids are "derived from 2 ppsm airborne LiDAR from Pictometry of 2010",
while the footprints themselves are a 2016 compilation
(sf13m_BldgFoot_withZ_20161005i). That is two defects in one column:

  A building finished after 2010 is drawn at whatever stood on its lot when the
  plane went over. Salesforce Tower, 326.1 m, returns 0.24 m — the scan caught
  the excavation.

  And a median is the wrong statistic for anything that tapers, steps or has a
  tower on a podium, because most of such a building's footprint sits under its
  wide base and the median lands there. The Transamerica Pyramid, 260.0 m,
  drew 67.8.

THE FIX. `5kya-mfst` Tall Building Inventory publishes `height_ft` for 180
named buildings. Where a row joins a footprint that is the height, and the
building carries FLAG_PUBHEIGHT (bit 5) so the page can say so. Where it does
not, the LiDAR median stands, and a building the roll dates after the 2010
flight carries FLAG_HEIGHT_STALE (bit 6) instead. `meta.heightFlags` ships both
counts, measured on the run, the way meta.yearFlags already does.

NOTHING IS DERIVED FROM STOREYS. The register publishes its own
`typ_story_height` and it does not agree with a published height divided by a
storey count, so neither is used to invent the other.

RESOLUTION IS PUBLISHED AGAINST PUBLISHED. A parcel can carry several
footprints and `max(area)` gives the published height to the podium, which is
how Spear Tower came to be drawn at 27.8 m against 172.2. The footprint whose
own LiDAR median is NEAREST the published height takes it, provided it reaches
half of it; where nothing reaches half — the building postdates the flight, so
none of it is in the 2010 grid — the largest takes it, because that is the only
thing left to say.

MEASURED, over the 155 rows that resolve: 124 heights raised, 31 lowered,
median change +7.1 m. The reductions are rooftop mechanical plant coming off a
LiDAR first-return surface that the register does not count; the largest is
680 Folsom, 13 storeys, published 166 ft, drawn 69.6 m.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, AND WHY. The MIN_H filter still tests the LiDAR value,
so two footprints a published height would rescue stay off the map — Salesforce
Tower at 0.24 m and 1190 Mission at 1.01 m. Rescuing them changes the RECORD
COUNT, and the count is the index every other per-building payload is keyed on:
roofs st/hf are packed nibbles of length (n+1)//2, paint idx/flag are one byte
per building, form-data indexes the stream directly, lod/blockof.npy caches an
assignment per building. San Francisco's tallest building is missing from the
map and that is written down rather than fixed by breaking four other files.

VERIFIED: cnt, vx, vy, xi, xn and useNames are BYTE-IDENTICAL before and after,
so building order and geometry have not moved and every index-keyed array still
aligns. 134 of the 166,166 height bytes change; the use class (u & 7) is
unchanged.

